    <innerclass refid="classCommunicate" prot="public">Communicate</innerclass>
    <briefdescription>
<para>Synchronous HTTP helper for service-to-service requests. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>Declares the <ref refid="classCommunicate" kindref="compound">Communicate</ref> class, a static-only wrapper around libcurl for making outbound HTTP calls. sendRequest builds a URL from a server, port and path, sends a JSON content-type header, and performs either a GET or a POST. Two private helpers do the work: handleResponse runs the cURL transfer and WriteCallback receives the returned bytes. All calls are blocking and failures are reported as an empty std::optional.</para>
<para>WHY THIS FILE MATTERS IN THE SERVER: When one part of the system needs to talk to another service over HTTP (for example a separate microservice), it uses <ref refid="classCommunicate" kindref="compound">Communicate</ref> instead of calling libcurl itself. That keeps the awkward C-style cURL setup in one place and gives callers a simple, safe interface: pass the address and data, get back an optional string. Without it, every caller would repeat handle setup, cleanup and error handling.</para>
<para>CONCEPTS TO LEARN (for beginners):<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>libcurl C API: <ulink url="https://curl.se/libcurl/c/">https://curl.se/libcurl/c/</ulink></para>
</listitem><listitem><para>std::optional: <ulink url="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/utility/optional">https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/utility/optional</ulink></para>
</listitem><listitem><para>Callbacks / function pointers: <ulink url="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/language/pointer#Pointers_to_functions">https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/language/pointer#Pointers_to_functions</ulink></para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
<para><simplesect kind="author"><para>Amr Osman <ulink url="mailto:dr3mro@gmail.com">dr3mro@gmail.com</ulink> </para>
</simplesect>
<simplesect kind="date"><para>2026-11-16 </para>
</simplesect>
</para>
